  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1206 AM CDT WED SEP 30 2026\n\nThis evening, surface ridging is situated across the East Coast\nand centered over VA/PA. In the upper levels, there is a weak\nridge across Mexico into East TX and LA pinned between a digging\nlow over AZ into the Desert Southwest and a large trough that\nextends down the Atlantic Seaboard including a weak low over FL\nand weak flow over AL. For today after a cool start in the middle\n50s to the lower 60s, we will creep back upward today across\nCentral AL with most locations topping out in the lower 90s. This\nwarming trend will continue through Friday with warm flow off of\nthe Gulf. The humidity will still be on the low side again on\nWednesday but a little higher than what was observed on Tuesday.\nOur further eastward surface ridge is setting up a southeast to\nsouth low level flow into AL for the rest of this week with\nonshore moisture return and increasing dew points. Moisture will\nneed to recover a bit before we can expect any significant rain\nchances. Very low rain chances return by late Thursday afternoon\nacross the southern counties.\n\nMore substantial chances hold off until this weekend ahead of an\napproaching front. There is a medium to high chance of this front\nstalling/moving very slowly. This will yield chances for some\nmuch needed rainfall Friday, over the weekend and continuing into\nthe beginning of next week. At this time, 2 to 3 inches of\nrainfall is most likely across much of Central Alabama before all\nis said and done Friday through Monday, but we can't rule out a\nfew isolated higher amounts as high as 4 inches. The higher rain \nchances will also help to hamper the warm up from progressing \nfurther over the weekend. Guidance is indicating that we should \nget enough of a push in the upper levels to get the surface front \nthrough on Monday so it can usher in some cooler readings back \ninto the area for the first half of next week.\n\n08",
